course: dictionary
lesson: 4942
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. «Showing off to impress someone often ends with a fall or an embarrassment.» Here impress means to make someone admire you. Ask yourself which meaning fits, then swap it into the sentence to check.
explain: Impress can also mean to make someone understand clearly how important something is. «Parents impress on children the importance of road safety.» Impress on means to repeat or stress something so that it really sinks in.
explain: «Saying "we had not considered that" impresses more than bluffing.» Bluffing means pretending to know more than you do. The clue tells you that honest, thoughtful answers win admiration, so impresses means makes a strong good impression.
fact: Impress means to make someone admire you, or to make them feel that something is very good.
fact: «Impress on» means to make someone understand clearly how important something is.
fact: «Be impressed by» means to feel admiration for someone or something.
fact: «Make an impression» means to affect someone strongly, often in a good way.
fact: Impress can also mean to press a mark or pattern into something soft, and in history it meant forcing men to serve in the navy.
exercise: «Showing off to impress someone often ends with a fall or an embarrassment.» What does impress mean here? | *To make someone admire you | To force someone to serve in the navy | To hurry someone along | To press a mark into something soft
exercise: «Parents impress on children the importance of road safety.» What does impress on mean here? | *To make children understand clearly how important road safety is | To give children a present | To ignore the rules | To press a pattern into clay
exercise: Ella says impress must always mean to make someone admire you. Is she right? | *No, it can also mean to make someone understand how important something is | Yes, it only ever means that | No, it always means to press down hard | Yes, and it also means to be sad
test: «Saying "we had not considered that" impresses more than bluffing.» Which word is closest in meaning to impresses here? | Annoys | *Wins admiration from | Confuses | Hides
test: In history, to impress men meant what? | To paint their portraits | *To force them to serve in the navy | To thank them for their help | To invite them to a party
exam: «Forest birds decorate their homes to impress a mate.» Which meaning of impress fits best here? | To press a mark into soft mud | To pay someone for their work | *To make someone admire you | To make someone sad
